  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Isaias bring rain, breezy conditions, \nand potential for isolated tornadoes today.\n\nRain associated with the remnants of Hurricane Isaias has overspread \nthe entire region this morning. Given 6 to 12 hours of persistent\nmoderate intensity rainfall, total precipitation amounts have \nlargely averaged between 1 to 2 inches. This also includes a few\nrandom pockets of 2 to 3 inches across portions of central \nVirginia around the Blue Ridge Mountains. Based on the radar \nappearance, this soaking rain is likely to continue through the \nremainder of the morning hours. Based on upstream activity, some\nof the storms over southwestern Virginia have a bit more \nvertical growth to them. Will see how this evolves and \nmaterializes while tracking toward a more stable thermodynamic \nenvironment across the Mid-Atlantic region. \n\nSynoptically not much has changed as the remnant mid-level \ncirculation of Isaias is now a large cutoff low over the TN Valley. \nForcing for ascent over the Mid-Atlantic is helping maintain \nthe coverage of rain. The interaction of the cutoff low to the \nwest and surface high to the east will create a large area of \nconvergence / broad frontal zone over the Mid-Atlantic. SPC \nMesoanalysis indicates that PWATs of 2\" are now pushing \nnorthward into southern Maryland.\n\nRain totals are generally still around 1.5-2.5\" across the area. \nOrographic effects of easterly winds into the Blue Ridge will result \nin 3-4\" of rain along the eastern slopes into the Piedmont. The \nFlood Watch in effect for those areas continues. Some of the high \nres models indicate the potential for enhanced rainfall over the DC \nmetro late this evening as a surface wave of low pressure approaches \nthen slows right around DC, before quickly departing to the east \nlate tonight. Any additional rain in the metro area could certainly \nlead to flooding concerns, so will continue to monitor model and \nradar trends to see if the Flood Watch need to be expanded. \n\nIn terms of the severe weather threat, SPC has now shifted the\nmain focus for more intense storms to I-64 and points southward.\nThis is where the Slight Risk has been shifted which is likely\ndriven by a stable air mass holding strong off to the north. \nOnce the main shield of rain departs to the north this afternoon,\nmost of the area enters a brief lull where we could see some \nbreaks in the clouds. There's a better chance for this to occur \nover Central VA to Southern MD. A wave of surface low pressure \nthen tracks through the area this afternoon. If this can take \nadvantage of any present instability, there is more than enough \nshear to fuel organized severe convection. The 12Z IAD sounding\nstill shows a large cyclonically looping hodograph (0-3 km \nstorm-relative helicity of almost 800 m2/s2), so the tornado \nthreat still exists, although cannot rule out a random damaging\nwind gust or two. The biggest question mark remains how much \ninstability can develop, and how far north that instability will\nreach. Right now, it appears the most favorable area is going \nto be east of the Blue Ridge and south of I-64.\n\nThe Wind Advisory remains through late this morning for the \nAlleghenies and Blue Ridge where gusts of 45-50 mph are expected. \nElsewhere, breezy conditions develop late this morning into the \nafternoon, with the strongest gusts around 20-30 mph. \n\nAnother round of steady rain is likely tonight as the cutoff low \naloft moves across the Mid-Atlantic. Precip comes to an end from \nwest to east Monday morning as the remnants of Isaias finally push \noffshore. Humid and warm conditions for Monday afternoon, though \nmost of the area is going to be dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures through mid week, then\ncooler conditions as moisture starved fronts move through.\n\nBrief ridging may bring high temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday\nto near 80 degrees. Low pressure passing north of the area will\nsend a cold front southward sometime on Wednesday. Most of the \nrain likely remains across Pennsylvania, although some showers \nmay sneak southward. \n\nThere remains significant model spread the progression of\nadditional fronts and upper troughs at the end of this week into\nnext weekend. However, the trend remains for cooler conditions\nas highs return closer to normal values for Thursday and\nFriday.",
